00; DN125\n- DN150\n- DN200 till DN350 (i.e., DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350)\n- DN400 till DN500 (DN400, DN450?, DN500) — hmm, \"till DN500\" could mean DN400, DN450, DN500 but pipe sizes typically go in steps. Actually DN sizes: DN400, DN450, DN500. But it could also just be DN400 and DN500. The revision history mentions \"DN200/DN250\" for revision 4, confirming DN250 exists. And \"DN600\".\n\nFor the model list, I should be careful about fabricating sizes not explicitly named. \"DN200 till DN350\" — the intermediate sizes (DN250, DN300) — DN250 is confirmed by revision 4 text (\"DN200/DN250\"). DN300 is not explicitly mentioned but \"till DN350\" implies the range includes intermediate DN sizes. For DN400 till DN500: DN450 might exist.\n\nTo be safe, I can list models as they appear in Table 4 with their row groupings:\n- DN50\n- DN65\n- DN80\n- DN100\n- DN125\n- DN150\n- DN200 till DN350 (group)\n- DN400 till DN500 (group)\n- DN600\n\nAlternatively, expand to individual sizes: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600. But DN300 and DN450 are not explicitly stated.\n\nGiven the table format \"DN200 till DN350\", I think the intended reading is all DN sizes in that range (DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350). The revision history mentions DN250, supporting the intermediate sizes. I'll expand to individual models but note the ranges. Hmm — risk of fabrication. Let me think.\n\nActually the safest representation: matrix_tables rows exactly as in Table 4 (preserving \"DN200 till DN350\" as the condition), and model_family models as the individual DN sizes where explicitly confirmed: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN350, DN400, DN500, DN600 — but DN300 and DN450 are in the \"till\" ranges but never explicitly named.\n\nHmm. For model_level characteristics in the R137 example, they map table rows to models. Here, table rows are groups. I could use model_level entries with conditions as model identifiers mat