list the individual sizes that are explicitly named plus the range groups. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The purpose of model_family.models is one entry per variant. For downstream usability, individual DN sizes are better. But fabrication risk...\n\nCompromise: In model_family.models, include individually: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 — and for the ones not explicitly written (DN300, DN450) they're implied by \"till\". I think that's over-reach.\n\nAlternative: I'll use the table conditions as model_ids: \"DN50\", \"DN65\", \"DN80\", \"DN100\", \"DN125\", \"DN150\", \"DN200 till DN350\", \"DN400 till DN500\", \"DN600\". This is faithful to the fragment. The matrix_tables rows will mirror this.\n\nHmm, but then model_level entries with 9 rows per attribute... The R137 example shows model_level values per model. I'll do model_level with model ids as the group conditions.\n\nActually wait — maybe cleaner: for the two range rows, split into individual DNs that are standard. Let me look at revision 2: \"Extension of line sizes\" — line sizes extended. Revision 5: \"reduced bore version with ratio 400\".\n\nI'll make a decision: expand to individual DN sizes (DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500) since that's the clear intent of \"till\", and it's standard DN nomenclature. If wrong about DN300/DN450, minor. Hmm...\n\nActually no. Let me be strict about non-fabrication. The table says \"DN200 till DN350\" — I cannot know if DN225, DN300 etc. are covered. I'll preserve the ranges. Final answer: use group conditions.\n\nFor model_level:\n- attribute: indicating_range, unit_symbol \"m³\":\n  - DN50: 99.999 — wait, is \"99.999\" a number 99.999 or \"99 999\" with dot as thousands separator? In European notation, 99.999 could be 99999. And \"9.999.999\" = 9,999,999 (nine million nine hundred ninety-nine thousand...). Given \"0,0001\" for verification scale interval (European decimal comma → 0.0001), the dots in \"99.999\" and \"9.999.999\" are thousands separators. So:\n  - DN50: 99.999 → 99999 m³ (indicating range 99,999 m³)\n  - DN65–DN125: 999.999 → 999999 m³\n  - DN150: 999.999 → 999999\n  - DN200–DN350: 9.999.999 → 9999999\n  - DN400–DN500: 9.999.999 → 9999999\n  - DN600: 99.999.999* → 99999999 (with footnote marker)\n\n  Yes: 99.999 = 99,999 (5 digits) makes sense for a DN50 meter with 0.0001 m³ scale interval (999999 intervals ≈ 10^6... Actually 99999/0.0001 = 999,990,000 intervals — that's too many. Hmm. R49 limits scale intervals... Actually for water meters, the indicating range... DN50: 99,999 m³ total with 0.0001 m³ resolution → 999,990,000 divisions? No wait — indicating range 99,999 m³ means the register can display up to 99,999 m³. Verification scale interval 0.0001 m³. The number of divisions is range/vmin = 99999/0.0001 = 999,990,000. That seems huge but it's a register display range, not accuracy-related. Common for water meter registers: 6-8 digits. 99.999 with dot as thousands separator = 5 digits (99,999). 999.999 = 6 digits (999,999). 9.999.999 = 7 digits (9,999,999). 99.999.999* = 8 digits (99,999,999). That's a classic register capacity progression: 5, 6, 6, 7, 7, 8 digits. Yes! This confirms dots are thousands separators.\n\n  So values: 99999, 999999, 999999, 9999999, 9999999, 99999999.\n\n- verification_scale_interval, unit m³:\n  - DN50: 0,0001 → 0.0001\n  - DN65–DN125: 0,001 → 0.001\n  - DN150: 0,01 → 0.01\n  - DN200–DN350: 0,01 → 0.01\n  - DN400–DN500: 0,1 → 0.1\n  - DN600: 0,1 → 0.1\n\n**Footnotes:** The asterisk footnote:\n\"\\* This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\"\n\nfootnotes: marker \"*\", text as above.\n\nThe DN600 indicating range value gets footnote_markers: [\"*\"].\n\n*